# Line endings.
#
# Every text file in this working tree is LF today, but the machine has
# core.autocrlf=true, so without an explicit eol the next checkout would rewrite
# all of them to CRLF - which is why the editor warns "this file uses LF but Git
# is configured to convert them to CRLF" on files nobody has touched. Pinning
# eol=lf stores LF and checks LF back out, so the tree stops changing under
# itself and a diff only ever shows real edits.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ...except the files a Windows program parses itself rather than Git.
# cmd.exe mis-handles LF-only labels and multi-line blocks, and these two ship
# inside the release zip, where a recipient double-clicks them.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Inno Setup script: ISCC is Windows-only and its own editor writes CRLF.
*.iss text eol=crlf

# Never normalise these - a byte changed in a PNG breaks the screenshot hashes
# in docs\screenshots\.demo that the privacy gate checks.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
